## Electrocatalytic methane oxidation to formate on magnesium based metal-organic frameworks
Menghuan Chen, Ximing Lv, Anxiang Guan, Chen Peng, Linping Qian, Gengfeng Zheng
Laboratory of Advanced Materials, Department of Chemistry and Shanghai Key Laboratory of Molecular Catalysis & Innovative Materials, Fudan University, Shanghai 200438, China
G R A P H I C A L A B S T R A C T
## A R T I C L E I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Article history:  Received 1 April 2022  Revised 27 April 2022  Accepted 9 May 2022  Available online 13 May 2022
Keywords:  Methane  Electrocatalysis  Metal–organic frameworks  Formate  Faradaic efficiency
The electrochemical methane oxidation reaction is a potential approach for upgrading the nature-abundant methane \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\right)\) into value-added chemicals, while the activity and selectivity have remained substantially low due to the extremely inert chemical property of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\). Inspired by the methane monooxygenase in nature, we demonstrated Mg-substituted metal–organic frameworks (Mg-MOF-74) containing a uniform distribution of Mg-oxo-Mg nodes as efficient catalytic sites. Compared to MgNi-MOF-74 and \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) without the Mg-oxo-Mg nodes, the Mg-MOF-74 presented a much enhanced \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) electrooxidation performance, with a unique selectivity of producing formate. The maximum Faradaic efficiency of all liquid products reached \(10.9\%\) at \(1.60~\mathrm{V}\) versus reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE), corresponding to the peak production rate of \(126.6~\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\).
