## 1. Introduction
With the continuous depletion of oil reserves and the increasing demand for diverse industrial chemicals, it becomes vital to utilize natural gas as an important future industrial feedstock [1,2]. Methane \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\right)\) is a highly inert molecule under ambient conditions. In addition to the direct combustion, the utilization of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) is mostly based on its pyrolysis into hydrogen and carbon monoxide (i.e., syngas) [3], followed by subsequent Fischer-Tropsch synthesis to produce hydrocarbons [4]. Substantial research efforts have been dedicated to investigate potential approaches for direct \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) upgrading into value-added chemicals such as methanol and olefins [5]. Transition metal ion-exchanged zeolites have been shown with good catalytic capabilities in converting \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) to methanol [6], while these reactions require high temperatures
tions. In addition to the direct combustion, the utilization of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) is mostly based on its pyrolysis into hydrogen and carbon monoxide (i.e., syngas) [3], followed by subsequent Fischer-Tropsch synthesis to produce hydrocarbons [4]. Substantial research efforts have been dedicated to investigate potential approaches for direct \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) upgrading into value-added chemicals such as methanol and olefins [5]. Transition metal ion-exchanged zeolites have been shown with good catalytic capabilities in converting \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) to methanol [6], while these reactions require high temperatures
and pressures [7]. On the other hand, many reports have demonstrated that electrochemical systems can be used for many catalytic reactions under ambient conditions [8–13]. For reducing the harsh conditions in the methane conversion, the electrochemical methane oxidation reaction \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\right)\) can represent a potential approach.
The electrochemical \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) oxidation reaction under mild conditions has been attracting increasing attention in recent years [14,15]. For instance, a \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\) - \(\mathrm{CuO}_{4}\) composite was reported to electrocatalyze \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) into \(n\) -propanol [16]. Bertazzoli and co-workers designed a hybrid oxide of \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) - \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) - \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\) that produces methanol and formic acid in \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\) [17]. However, the product yields in these works were low and the selectivities were poor, and the use of expensive noble metals also inhibits the future large-scale deployment. Thus, it is important to develop earth-abundant element-based \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\) electrocatalysts, which can allow for effective activation of \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}\) bonds and at the same time limit the overoxidation toward \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\).
In the nature, the core structure of Methane monooxidase enzyme contains an uncoordinated metal–oxygen cluster, which can selectively oxidize \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) into methanol [18–20]. This feature of metal–oxygen clusters can be leveraged to inspire designs of new \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\) catalysts. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a large family of organic compounds with 3-dimensional porous structures and periodic metal–oxygen nodes, which can feature as active sites during catalysis [21–23]. Among them, the MOF-74 series have carboxylic acid ligands with metal-oxide-metal nodes. Several studies have reported that MOFs catalysts containing non-noble metal–oxygen structures can present efficient oxidation performances of organic molecules [24–26]. To endow them with potential \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\) capabilities, we proposed that the non-noble metallic sites in MOFs can be rationally introduced with unsaturated coordination of metallic nodes in MOFs.
Herein, we developed a magnesium \(\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}\right)\) -based metal–organic frameworks (Mg-MOF-74), in which \(\mathtt{M g}\) ions were connected with oxygen atoms to form Mg-oxo-Mg functional nodes. The Mg-MOF74 electrocatalyst exhibited excellent production of formate \((66.07\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and acetate \((60.56\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) , as well as a corresponding total Faradaic efficiency (FE) of \(10.9\%\) , suggesting the potential of designing new bio-inspired catalytic sites for \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) electrocatalytic oxidation.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals and materials
Magnesium nitrate hexahydrate \((\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) , Sinopharm Chemical), nickel acetate tetrahydrate \((\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) , Sinopharm Chemical), 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DOT}\) , Aladdin Corporation), tetrahydrofuran (THF, Sinopharm Chemical), sodium hydroxide (NaOH, Sinopharm Chemical), potassium hydroxide (KOH, Merck), platinum carbon (Pt-C, \(60\%\) , Johnson Matthey), Nafion D-520 dispersion (53 w/w in water and 1-propanol, Alfa Aesar), acetone (AR, Sinopharm Chemical), methanol \((>\!99.9\%\) , Aladdin Corporation), methane \((99\%)\) , and argon \((99.9\%)\) .
## 2.2. Synthesis of Mg-MOF-74, MgNi-MOF-74, and \(M g(O H)_{2}\)
The synthesis process of Mg-MOF-74 was modified based on a previous report [27]. In brief, \(0.384\,\mathrm{g}\) of \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was added to \(10\;\mathrm{mL}\) of THF and \(3~\mathrm{mL}\) of deionized (DI) water to form a homogeneous solution, followed by adding \(3~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{NaOH}\) solution. The mixture was stirred at room temperature for \(^{2\mathrm{~h~}}\) . Afterwards, the suspension was mixed with \(0.149\,\mathrm{g}\) of \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DOT}\) , and then transferred into a \(50{\-}\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined steel container and heated at 110
\(^{\circ}C\) for \(72\,\mathrm{h}\) . The products were filtered and washed with DI water several times. Finally, the dark yellow substance was dried at 80 \(^\circ C\) in air for \(^{12\mathrm{~h~}}\) to obtain Mg-MOF-74. Similarly, MgNi-MOF-74 was prepared using the same method, except for the partial replacement of \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) with \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}\) . The initial molar ratio of \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) salt precursors was 2:1.
\(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) was synthesized by alkaline treatment of Mg-MOF-74. Typically, \(200~\mathrm{mg}\) of as-synthesized Mg-MOF-74 was mixed with \(100\;\mathrm{mL}\) of \(1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{NaOH}\) and stirred at \(50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) . The final suspension was gathered via centrifugation, washed with DI water, and dried in air at \(80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) overnight to collect the product.
## 2.3. Electrode fabrication
Typically, \(10\,\mathrm{~mg~}\) of the synthesized catalysts and \(50\,\textrm{\textmu L}\) of Nafion were dispersed in \(2.5~\mathrm{mL}\) of acetone by ultrasonication to generate a homogenous catalyst ink. The anode was prepared by drop-casting the catalyst ink on carbon paper with a gas diffusion layer (GDL). The electrode was dried and kept in vacuum for the subsequent electrochemical measurements.
## 3. Results and discussion
As inspired by the function of Methane monooxidase [28,29], the metal-oxo-metal structure can play an important role in catalyzing \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) partial oxidation. This metal-oxo-metal structure was mimicked in our study as the catalytic site to improve the activity and selectivity of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\) . Mg-MOF-74 is known to have a periodic homogeneous Mg-oxo-Mg structure [30], and the unsaturated Mg site is prone to bind adsorbates (Fig. 1a). According to the previous report [31], MOF-74 presents great capability for methane adsorption, thus making it potential to serve as a \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\) catalyst. The Mg-MOF-74 sample was prepared by a hydrothermal approach (Experimental section). To illustrate the key role of the Mg-oxo-Mg structure in Mg-MOF-74 for \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\) , two control samples, including Ni-substituted Mg-MOF-74 (designated as MgNi-MOF-74) and Mg \(\left(\mathrm{OH}\right)_{2}\) , were also synthesized and compared (Experimental section). Due to the similar sizes between \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) \(\left(69\,\mathrm{pm}\right)\) and \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) ions \((72\,\mathrm{pm})\) , the strain effect in MgNi-MOF-74 should be only marginal [32].
\(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) [35]. The Mg 2p binding energies in Mg-MOF-74 and MgNi-MOF-74 were observed at 50.24 and 50.41 eV, respectively (Fig. 2b), corresponding to the existence of \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) [36]. The O 1s spectra of Mg-MOF-74 and MgNi-MOF-74 were deconvoluted into three main peaks at 530.96, 531.67, and 533.16 eV (Fig. 2c). The first one was assigned to oxygen atoms of Mg–O bonds \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{c}}\right)\), and the other two were ascribed to oxygen atoms of hydroxyl species \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{a}}\right)\) and adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{b}}\right)\), respectively [37,38]. As for \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\), the peak of O 1 s appeared at 531.15 eV, corresponding to oxygen atoms in hydroxide anions [39]. The main peaks of Ni 2p in MgNi-MOF-74 were located at 855.97 and 873.54 eV (Fig. 2d), assigning
to Ni \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and Ni \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) , respectively [40], and two additional satellite peaks were observed at 861.88 and \(880.08~\mathrm{{eV}}\) . The infrared spectra were conducted to characterize the chemical interaction between metal ions and organic ligands in these samples. The adsorption peaks of Mg-MOF-74 were different from its \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DOT}\) precursor (Fig. S4, with the detailed description in the figure caption).
The electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\) measurements were then evaluated for these three catalysts under room temperature and ambient pressure. All the applied potentials were converted and reported as the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) scale in this work.
The cyclic voltammetry (CV) curve of Mg-MOF-74 from 1.0 to \(2.2\,\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE was first conducted in 1 M KOH with a scan rate of \(10\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) under Ar or \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) (Fig. S5a). A weak oxidation peak was observed in the CV curve at \(\sim1.6\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE, which was attributed to the oxidation of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\). The Tafel plots derived from LSV curves showed that the slope of Mg-MOF-74 was \(397\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\), and the slopes of MgNi-MOF-74 and \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) were 444 and \(295\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\), respectively (Fig. S5b). The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves were then measured at the range between 1.2 and \(2.0\;\mathrm{V}\) in Ar-saturated or \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\)-saturated electrolytes (Fig. S6). For each of these three catalysts, the current density in the \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\)-saturated electrolyte was higher than that in the Ar-saturated electrolyte. At \(2.0\;\mathrm{V}\), the total current density of Mg-MOF-74, MgNi-MOF-74, and \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) reached 10.5, 10.9, and \(11.8\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), respectively (Fig. 3a).
The chronoamperometry tests were then conducted to investigate the \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\) product distributions at different potentials, from which the gas and liquid products were characterized by gas chromatography and nuclear magnetic resonance (Methods in the Supporting Information), respectively. Compared with the gas effluent before the reaction, there were no obvious gas products in \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\) (Fig. S7). For MgNi-MOF-74 and \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\), acetate was the only product. While for Mg-MOF-74, both formate and acetate were observed as the electrolysis products (Fig. S8), and the total FE of formate and acetate reached a peak value of \(10.9\%\) at \(1.60\;\mathrm{V}\) (Fig. 3b). The maximum \(\mathrm{FE}_{\mathrm{formate}}\) reached \(4.9\%\) at \(1.60\;\mathrm{V}\), corresponding to a partial current density of \(30\;\upmu\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and a formate production rate of \(66.07\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Fig. 3c). In the meantime, the \(\mathrm{FE}_{\mathrm{acetate}}\) also reached its maximum value of \(6.0\%\), with an acetate partial current density of \(36\;\upmu\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and an acetate production rate of \(60.56\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Fig. 3d). Combining together, the Mg-MOF-74 electrocatalyst exhibited the highest total FE \((10.9\%)\) and production rates \((126.6\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) of liquid products among those three samples (Fig. 4a). These data are comparable to the best \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\) results in the literature to date (Table S1 in the Supporting Information).
