Furthermore, the electrochemical stability of the Mg-MOF-74 catalyst were investigated by the long-time electrolysis. At an applied potential of \(1.6\;\mathrm{V}\), the Mg-MOF-74 catalyst showed a slow decrease of the total current density over 10,000 s (Fig. S9), suggesting a reasonably good stability for \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\). After the \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) electrooxidation, the structure of the Mg-MOF-74 catalyst was recharacterized by XRD (Fig. S1b, red curve), XPS (Fig. S10, where the standard binding energies of \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) were listed in Table S2), and SEM (Fig. S11). Those data showed that both the crystal and electronic structures of Mg-MOF-74 were well retained after the electrolysis, confirming its good electrochemical stability.
The mechanism of the electrochemical \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{OR}\) on Mg-MOF-74 was then investigated to study the reaction pathway of producing formate. The methanol \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\right)\) electrooxidation test was conducted in 1 M KOH (detailed methods in the Supporting Information). The current density of LSV curves showed an enhancement after the addition of methanol (Fig. S12a). A redox peak appeared at \(1.70\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE in the CV curves (Fig. S12b), which was ascribed to the oxidation of methanol [41]. Based on the nuclear magnetic resonance analysis, the product was formate with a corresponding FE of \(25\%\) (Fig. 1b). According to previous
studies on the methanol electrooxidation, the methoxy group was attributed to the first intermediate [42]. Thus, the generation of formate was described below, as in the previous reports [42].
\[
\begin{array}{rl}&{\mathrm{CH}_4-2\mathrm{e}^-+20\mathrm{H}^-\longrightarrow\mathrm{CH}_3\mathrm{O}^-+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}}\\&{\mathrm{CH}_3\mathrm{O}^--\mathrm{e}^-+\mathrm{OH}^-\longrightarrow\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}}\\&{\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{O}^--\mathrm{e}^-+\mathrm{OH}^-\longrightarrow\mathrm{CHO}+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}}\\&{\mathrm{CHO}^--\mathrm{e}^-+\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{ads}}+\mathrm{OH}^-\longrightarrow\mathrm{HCOO}^-+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}}\end{array}
\]
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we developed a Mg-based MOF-74 catalyst with coordinated Mg-oxo-Mg as the active site for electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) oxidation under ambient conditions. Due to the Mg ions on Mgoxo-Mg nodes [30], the Mg-MOF-74 electrocatalyst presented a good selectivity for producing formate and acetate, with maximum total FE of \(10.9\%\) and production rate of \(126.6\ \upmu\mathrm{mol\cdot h^{-1}\cdot g^{-1}}\) at \(1.60\ \mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE, substantially exceeding the MgNi-MOF-74 and \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) counterparts and was also comparable to the best literature reports. The unique Mg-oxo-Mg sites in Mg-MOF-74 play a key role in generating formate during the electrochemical methane oxidation reactions. Our work suggests new opportunities for designing new efficient catalysts for electrochemical methane oxidation under ambient conditions.
